.TH CROS-TYPEC-SELECTOR 8 "September 2026" "cros-typec-selector 0.1.0"
.SH NAME
cros-typec-selector \- select generic USB-C data modes on AP-driven Chrome EC systems
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B cros-typec-selector inspect
.RI [ PORT ]
.br
.B cros-typec-selector decide
.RI [ PORT ]
.br
.B cros-typec-selector reconcile
.RI [ PORT ]
.RI [ \-\-live ]
.br
.B cros-typec-selector daemon \-\-live
.SH DESCRIPTION
The selector reconciles Linux Type-C connector-class topology and chooses USB4, Thunderbolt compatibility, DisplayPort, or ordinary USB in that order. It derives decisions from USB-PD identity and alternate-mode data, not peripheral identifiers.
.PP
Inspect, decide, and reconcile are read-only unless
.B \-\-live
is explicit. The daemon requires that flag. Udev events are treated as hints and cause fresh enumeration. Discovery and mode-transition retries are bounded and cancelled on topology changes.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-\-sysfs " PATH"
Use an alternate Type-C class root, primarily for fixture inspection.
.TP
.B \-\-discovery-expired
For diagnostic decisions, show the fail-safe result after the discovery deadline.
.TP
.B \-\-live
Permit connector-class mode writes.
.SH SECURITY BOUNDARY
The program never authorizes Thunderbolt devices, invokes boltctl, changes the boltd store, or creates tunnels. Router authorization remains exclusively owned by boltd.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /sys/class/typec
Linux Type-C connector class.
.TP
.I /sys/class/chromeos/cros_ec/ap_mode_entry
Observable Chrome EC AP-driven entry capability.
